WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.731
Change one thing at a time, and rerun everything.

2
00:00:03.011 --> 00:00:06.744
The temptation after a bad output is to rewrite half the prompt.

3
00:00:07.024 --> 00:00:07.963
Resist it.

4
00:00:08.243 --> 00:00:19.165
If you change the role, add three examples, and reorder the instructions at once, and the score goes up, you don’t know which change helped, or whether one of them hurt and was masked by the others.

5
00:00:19.445 --> 00:00:26.165
This is ordinary experimental design: vary one factor, hold the rest fixed, compare against the baseline.

6
00:00:26.445 --> 00:00:30.968
Two more rules follow from week 1’s lesson that output varies between runs.

7
00:00:31.248 --> 00:00:36.368
First, rerun the whole test set after every change, not just the case you were fixing.

8
00:00:36.648 --> 00:00:43.837
Prompts are tangled: a sentence that fixes the ambiguous-ticket case can make the model over-cautious on three others.

9
00:00:44.117 --> 00:00:51.669
Track fixes (failed before, pass now) and regressions (passed before, fail now) per case, not only the overall pass rate.

10
00:00:51.949 --> 00:00:54.360
Second, be suspicious of small differences.

11
00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.848
If a case flips between pass and fail when you rerun the same version, a one-case improvement is noise.

12
00:01:01.128 --> 00:01:07.507
Studio 3 covers how many repeats you need; for now, rerun anything that looks like a marginal win.

13
00:01:07.787 --> 00:01:09.899
Finally, keep your test set honest.

14
00:01:10.179 --> 00:01:17.219
If you tune a prompt against the same 20 cases for a week, you will eventually fit those 20 cases rather than the task.

15
00:01:17.499 --> 00:01:23.301
Hold some cases back, add new ones from real failures, and never delete a case because it fails.
